WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Count the last five books you finished.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:08.840
How many changed how you think, decide,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.920
communicate, or succeed? If the answer

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:14.720
is none, you're not alone. Most people

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.360
read books and forget them, but the top

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:20.480
1% read to change something and to

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.560
become dangerously smart. For those of

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.520
you who are new here, I've been the CEO,

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.920
board member, and investor in

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.640
billion-dollar tech companies, and I

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.080
want to show you a system that helps you

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.640
read better,

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.360
learn faster, remember what matters, and

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.960
use AI to turn what you've read into

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.880
something that helps you win the room.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.000
So, let's get started. There are three

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.320
myths we carry around about reading,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.200
remembering, and learning. The first

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:54.040
myth is a story we tell ourselves about

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:56.600
how we learn. I am a visual learner. I

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:59.800
need to hear something to retain it. I

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:03.320
only learn by doing. Now, these all feel

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.160
true, but researchers across four major

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:08.840
universities spent years looking for

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.840
some evidence for this. Do visual

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.600
learners actually learn better visually?

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.040
Do auditory learners actually retain

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.720
more by listening? Turns out there was

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.320
very little evidence that our preferred

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.400
learning style improves [music] our

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.840
learning and retention. Now, this

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:31.120
surprising myth costs us dearly because

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.960
that self-inflicted limit that we put on

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.080
ourselves becomes our identity. The

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.800
label creates the ceiling. Now, the

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:42.800
second myth is even more subtle.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.080
If a book explains something clearly, we

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.920
assume that we understood it clearly.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.240
Our brains are very good at lying to us.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.600
In one study from Yale University,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.840
people were asked [music] how well did

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:58.720
they understand everyday objects like

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:01.440
bicycles or zippers or toilets, and they

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.240
all felt confident until they had to

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.600
explain step-by-step [music]

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.520
how those objects actually functioned.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.080
Then, their confidence completely

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.400
collapsed. This is the illusion of

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:16.480
fluency, and those myths create three

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.920
interesting reading traps. There is the

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.720
highlighter trap, where you mark the

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.101
sentence and mistake that marking for

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.160
memory. There is the summary trap,

00:02:24.101 --> 00:02:26.560
>> [music]

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.686
>> where you make perfect notes that you

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.280
will never ever read again. There is

00:02:28.686 --> 00:02:32.480
[music] the completion trap. So, the

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.959
book is done, but nothing has been

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.280
formed inside you. But now comes the

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:40.480
biggest myth of all, and it comes from

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.240
AI. If AI can summarize the book, why do

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.120
I need to read it? But you know, AI has

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:48.000
already read almost all books on this

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.080
planet. You're not going to grow your

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.720
muscles if you haven't done a single

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.080
push-up yourself. So, ask AI to

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.840
summarize a book, and it will do a fine

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.280
job instantly, and you feel like you've

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.440
read it, but you haven't wrestled with

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.640
its core [music] ideas. Can you explain

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:08.760
it? Can you remember it? Can you use it?

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:11.680
You know, the more AI makes reading feel

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.560
optional, the more we need [music] a

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.120
better way to read. That's the path to

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.200
the top 1%. If you want to change the

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.400
way you think, you have to [music]

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.720
change the way you read. And that's the

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.920
system we're going to build here in this

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.400
video. Reading was never just about

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.560
books. It was about training for

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.320
complexity. How you follow an argument,

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.840
hold two opposing ideas in your [music]

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.320
head, change your mind when the new

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.720
evidence comes, and form an opinion that

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.239
actually belongs to [music] you. The

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.440
difference between a passive reader and

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:48.519
a serious one is not speed reading

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.480
anymore. It is [music] your position.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:53.520
Are you sitting outside the book,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.280
watching the author think, or are you

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.240
becoming an actor in the story that is

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.680
unfolding in front of you? That's the

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.480
system you need to build. I call that

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:06.040
framework actor, and the framework has

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.200
five moves inside it. We'll go through

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.320
each step in detail through the video,

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:13.560
but here's something to keep in mind. In

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:16.959
this framework, AI belongs inside of

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:20.799
each move, but never as a shortcut. You

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:24.160
are still the actor. AI is the sidekick.

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:27.560
That changes the entire experience.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.080
Let's start with A for aim. Most people

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:33.480
read a book as tourists. The best

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.720
readers read it as a spy. In 2008,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:38.520
Lin-Manuel Miranda picked up the

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:41.640
biography of Alexander Hamilton. He was

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:43.960
on vacation, and the book was 800 pages

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.640
long. And when he was reading it, he was

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.440
just looking for something to read on

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:50.320
the beach, but Miranda had a lifelong

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.360
obsession that he carried everywhere.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:57.160
Hip-hop, immigration, and this idea that

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:59.520
words are how people with nothing build

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.280
everything. That obsession was his

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.040
mission. And when that passion connected

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.400
with that book, with Hamilton's story,

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.640
something inside of Miranda ignited. The

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.840
mission changed the material, and

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:16.720
Miranda was inspired to write a Broadway

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.919
musical called Hamilton, which of course

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.360
became one of the most successful

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:24.880
Broadway shows in history. Same pages,

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:27.680
different mission, different impact.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.560
Your purpose turns any reading from

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:33.600
consumption to construction. So, before

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:38.200
you read, just write one sentence. I am

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:40.800
reading this book because I need to XYZ.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.600
That blank is your mission. You might

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.360
read one book for leadership principles,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.520
another as a travelogue, another to

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:51.720
understand your relationship with money,

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:54.320
but without that one sentence, the book

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:57.480
will decide what matters. With that

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.360
sentence, with your purpose, you decide

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.960
what to hunt for. And what if you don't

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.440
know what the mission is yet? Well,

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.880
that's where AI can help. You can always

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.040
ask before you read, "I'm about to read

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.640
this book. Give me three questions that

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:13.760
I should carry into it, so I read with

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.720
purpose, not just passively." Or reverse

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:19.360
the whole idea. "I'm trying to deal with

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.600
a dysfunctional team at work. Which book

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.160
would serve that purpose, and what

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.880
questions should I carry into it?" You

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.520
are still the actor.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:31.440
AI is the [music] framer. It helps you

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.840
articulate what you need before the book

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.320
starts shaping it for you. So, before

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.280
your next serious book, or an article,

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.600
or a memo, or a research paper, write

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:45.480
the mission statement down. Just one

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:49.320
sentence. And also remember that a great

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:52.600
book is nothing but a doorway to another

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:55.200
great book. Now, C stands for compress.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:58.160
Don't read a book to collect more, read

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.760
it to carry less. Elon Musk once

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.880
described knowledge as a tree. [music]

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.480
Before you collect the leaves, you need

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.919
to see where the trunk is and how the

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:10.339
branches are formed. Otherwise, the

00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:10.800
leaves will have nothing to hold on to.

00:07:10.339 --> 00:07:13.280
>> [music]

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.234
>> It's a very simple metaphor, but it

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.480
explains why so much of what we read

00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:18.840
>> [music]

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.820
>> disappears from our mind. Because we

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.960
don't focus on seeing the tree

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.320
>> [music]

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:26.520
>> or the trunk. So, what is the trunk? It

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:28.560
is the book's core central idea. The

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.880
idea that holds everything else

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.320
together. The branches are the major

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:37.080
chapters or arguments, and the leaves

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:39.520
are the examples, the quotes, the

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.800
stories, and the details. And you know,

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.920
most people read as if they're just

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:46.880
collecting leaves. A great quote here, a

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:50.040
a highlight a sentence there, a

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.600
screenshot, a clever paragraph or a

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:55.960
photograph, but they miss the

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.520
load-bearing idea. They miss the trunk.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:59.760
This is compression. [music]

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.480
A book is not meant to be carried page

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:06.000
by page. It has to be compressed into

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:08.120
something your mind can hold.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.600
That does not mean dummying it down.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.910
That just means that you have to find a

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.040
structure that makes all the details

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.560
>> [music]

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:17.000
>> meaningful. Aim is about finding the

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.440
root. Compress is about finding the

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:23.600
trunk. Now, some books have very [music]

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:26.880
clear trunks, like Atomic Habits or Grit

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.960
or Start With Why, and some others are

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.440
harder, like Zen and the Art of

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.680
Motorcycle Maintenance, The Innovator's

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.560
Dilemma, or Narcissism and Goldman.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:39.320
These are great books, but they're not

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.960
very easy to understand. In those cases,

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.160
when you can't see the trunk very

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.480
clearly, you can go back to AI because

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.040
AI becomes your interpreter.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:52.800
Ask, "I think the load-bearing idea in

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.520
this book is X. Check my interpretation.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.520
What did I miss? What did I

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:58.800
misunderstand? What did I overstate?

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:01.040
Because, you know, if you look at a

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:04.160
tree, leaves can't exist without

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.720
branches and branches need a trunk. So,

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.920
if you only collect leaves, you won't be

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.960
able to see the shape of the book, the

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.720
shape of the tree. So, after each

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:17.120
reading, write a short version of the

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:19.400
key idea, the takeaway, and then go back

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.240
to AI and challenge your interpretation.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:23.880
And that's the compression you need

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.960
before you move to the next step. Next

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.920
is T for test. The best readers don't

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:32.360
read to agree. They read to find what

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.960
they want to reject. In a classic

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:39.040
Stanford study, people with strong views

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:41.680
on the death penalty were shown mixed

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.720
evidence. Now, you would think that

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.840
mixed evidence would make people feel

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:49.400
more balanced. It did the exact

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.680
opposite. People attacked the evidence

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:54.040
they disliked and praised the evidence

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.760
they already agreed with and walked away

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:58.720
even more convinced that they were right

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.280
all along. And that is why one of the

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:03.360
most interesting reading habits is what

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.680
I heard about from Bill Gates. He has

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:08.040
said that when he disagrees with a book,

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.760
he writes even more feverishly in the

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.800
margins. He does not treat disagreement

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:16.320
as a reason to quit. He makes a point to

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:19.240
think harder about those ideas. Now,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:22.000
think about us. Most of us underline and

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:24.000
highlight all the parts that flatter our

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.880
point of view and we discard all the

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:29.560
ideas that don't agree with us. I do it

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.600
all the time. So, read like you are a

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:35.400
spy in the enemy's camp. You're not

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:38.160
there to surrender, of course. You're

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:41.160
there to steal information you find

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.640
useful. A serious reader can disagree

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.839
with the author and still

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:48.800
leave with something very valuable

00:10:45.839 --> 00:10:51.640
because testing is where reading becomes

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:54.600
self-discovery. The moment you reject a

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:57.360
paragraph, ask whether you found a flaw

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.880
in the book or it was bruising your own

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.240
ego or your belief system. And you can

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.200
always ask, why did that bother me so

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.720
much?

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:09.400
What belief am I protecting or holding

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.680
on to? Where's the author [music] right?

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:14.360
Where's he or she wrong?

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:17.520
What would I have to believe if I were

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:20.200
arguing the opposite? This is where AI

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:22.640
becomes super useful as an opponent. The

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:25.640
best thing AI can do is to become your

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.440
sparring partner. Ask AI, "Challenge my

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.520
interpretation. Find the hidden

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:32.000
assumption that I'm making. Give me your

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.680
best counter-argument and describe a

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.200
situation where this advice will fail.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:38.238
All of that helps you embrace that book

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:38.360
more gently.

00:11:38.238 --> 00:11:40.120
>> [music]

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.400
>> You know, if a book only makes you

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:44.720
comfortable, it's not going to change

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.280
you. Next is own.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:50.160
At Washington University in St. Louis,

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:52.760
researchers gave students short reading

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.920
passages. Some students were told to

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:57.640
read the passages again and again and

00:11:54.920 --> 00:12:00.080
others were told to look away and try to

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:02.360
recall what they had just read. Now, the

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:04.680
first group felt very confident in the

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:07.440
short run, but in the long run, the

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:09.920
other group was able to recall much more

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.520
clearly. So, looking away from what

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:15.680
you're reading and then rehashing it in

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:17.840
your own way is one of the best ways to

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:19.640
own it. The weird thing about reading a

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:22.120
book again is that you'll feel

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:24.800
comfortable because it feels familiar,

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:28.000
but the only way to own it is not to

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:30.720
just reread it, but to relive it in your

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.360
own words. Now, the second way to own it

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:36.800
is to connect it with something real in

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.640
your life. Maybe a meeting, a mistake, a

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:42.240
company, a situation, a conversation, a

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:45.360
person, an old belief. Give it some

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.960
meaning because meaning gives memory a a

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:50.680
place to live. And the third test, which

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:53.360
is one of my favorite ones, is the

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:55.160
simplest. Teach it to someone. Even if

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:58.320
you're teaching it to a wall. If you

00:12:55.160 --> 00:13:00.760
cannot teach it, you do not own it yet.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:04.000
By teaching it, you're moving the idea

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:06.440
from the page into your mind. So, after

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:08.720
every book or a long form article that

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:11.320
you read, explain the book in a

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:13.520
paragraph or two just for yourself. And

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:15.760
again, this is where AI becomes a

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:18.480
fantastic coach. You can ask your

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:21.520
favorite AI, "Help me explain this idea

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:24.320
in plain English. Connect it to one

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:27.760
business example or one personal

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:30.920
experience or one analogy." Anything you

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:34.040
can do to contextualize it and own it.

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:37.680
Or do what I do sometimes, I teach it to

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:40.760
AI and ask if I'm hitting all the right

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:43.600
notes. Buying a book means that you own

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:47.040
the object. The hard part is to own

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:49.840
what's inside it. R is run. Books have

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:52.760
always been civilization's software

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:55.840
updates, right? Every major religion has

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:57.640
a core text. The Bible, the Gita, the

00:13:55.840 --> 00:14:00.760
Quran, the Torah. [music]

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:04.240
Newton's Principia rewired minds toward

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:07.160
science. Mein Kampf rewired minds toward

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.880
violence. So, a book can build a future

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:11.560
or it can burn it down. So, the question

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:14.680
isn't whether a book [music] changes the

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:18.000
world. It can. The question is whether

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:21.000
it changes you. You know, MIT's motto is

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.560
mind and hand because thinking isn't

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:26.560
enough and it's not finished until it

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:27.880
helps you build something real that

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.160
could change something in the real

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:32.480
world. A communication book should

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.320
change a conversation. A money book

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:37.080
should change a decision. A leadership

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:39.880
book should change how you run your

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:41.400
team. I'll give you my example. When I

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:43.920
read this book called Crucial

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:47.160
Conversations, it did more than just

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:50.040
inspire me. It changed how I behaved and

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:53.040
what I noticed during real crucial

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:55.640
conversations. I became more aware of

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:59.440
three things. Whether I was making the

00:14:55.640 --> 00:15:02.839
room emotionally safe for everyone.

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:04.480
Whether I mastered my own story that I

00:15:02.839 --> 00:15:07.640
was telling myself.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:10.360
And whether we were building a shared

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:13.720
pool of meaning. These are all concepts

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:16.680
from the book. Now, am I really good at

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:19.080
crucial conversations? Of course not.

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:21.920
I've made plenty of mistakes and

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:24.600
mishandled many crucial conversations

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.840
and paid dearly for some of them, but

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:29.280
that's not the point. The true power of

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:31.480
the book is that it can interrupt the

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:34.560
way you used to run your life. It gives

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:36.760
you awareness, and that's where changes

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:40.360
happen. And this is also where AI

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:43.840
becomes your action companion. Ask it,

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:47.640
"Turn this idea into one decision, one

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:50.840
rule, one checklist, one experiment,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:52.080
anything you can do to change words into

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.440
actions."

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:56.040
And by the way, if you'd like the

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:58.240
frameworks we covered in this video,

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.160
please sign up for my newsletter. Link

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:03.760
is below. It's free.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:05.760
And you know, in the age of AI, everyone

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:06.791
is going to have access to the same

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.280
summaries.

00:16:06.791 --> 00:16:09.160
>> [music]

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:10.640
>> Everyone's going to ask the same key

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:13.080
takeaways.

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:14.360
Everyone will generate the same polished

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:17.640
notes.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:20.320
The edge is no longer your access to

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:22.760
intelligence. The real edge is what you

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:26.160
bring to it as a human being.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:29.400
Your judgment, your taste, and a point

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:33.920
of view that is uniquely yours.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:35.520
But there's also a much deeper benefit

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:38.000
in reading.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:41.520
Because after a while,

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:44.360
the books you read start reading you.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:46.080
And by that, I mean they help you reveal

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:50.000
the stories that [music] you tell

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:53.600
yourself, the fears you protect,

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:55.920
the assumptions that you've inherited,

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:59.040
and the parts of life that you still

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:01.360
don't understand. You know, a great book

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:04.720
works like a great song.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:07.880
You hear it once, and you enjoy it, then

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:11.680
you hear it again years later, and

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:13.959
somehow that song knows exactly where

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:16.000
you are in your life.

00:17:13.959 --> 00:17:19.120
The song didn't change.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:22.360
You did. And that's why yet to meet a

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:25.880
great leader who does not read.

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:27.959
Because the deeper you read, the better

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:29.920
you start reading people.

00:17:27.959 --> 00:17:32.160
You read the room better.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:35.920
You read silence better.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:37.560
That's why serious leaders are serious

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:39.280
readers.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.280
I'll see you next week.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:42.920
Thank you.

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:42.920
And I love you.

